                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="main">
                <div style="margin-bottom:16px">
                    <div style="font-size:.75em;font-weight:600;color:var(--text-muted);margin-bottom:4px">Module 2 &middot; NEET Chemistry</div>
                    <h1 style="font-size:1.5em;font-weight:900">Inorganic Chemistry</h1>
                    <div style="color:var(--text-sec);font-size:.85em;margin-top:4px">Periodic table, chemical bonding, coordination compounds, s/p/d/f blocks.</div>
                    <div style="display:flex;gap:10px;margin-top:8px;flex-wrap:wrap">
                        <span style="font-size:.75em;color:var(--text-muted)">Periodic Table · Coordination · s-block · p-block · d-block · f-block</span>
                    </div>
                    <div style="display:flex;gap:8px;margin-top:10px">
                        <button onclick="window.print()" class="download-btn">&#x1f4c4; Download PDF</button>
                        <a href="../../mock-tests/index.html" class="download-btn">&#x1f3af; Take Mock Test</a>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="lesson-body"><h3>Learning Objectives</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Classify elements based on electronic configuration and predict periodic trends in atomic radius, ionization enthalpy, electronegativity, and electron affinity for NEET problem-solving</li>
    <li>Analyse anomalous behaviour of second-period elements and diagonal relationships in the s- and p-blocks</li>
    <li>Describe the chemistry of hydrogen, its isotopes, hydrides, water hardness, hydrogen peroxide structure and reactions</li>
    <li>Recall general characteristics, trends, and important compounds of s-block (alkali and alkaline earth metals) with emphasis on anomalous Li and Be</li>
    <li>Systematically compare p-block groups 13 to 18: trends in inert-pair effect, allotropy, catenation, and oxidising power</li>
    <li>Rationalize electronic configurations, oxidation states, magnetic and catalytic properties of d- and f-block elements</li>
    <li>Apply IUPAC nomenclature of coordination compounds, identify isomerism types, and explain bonding using VBT and CFT</li>
    <li>Outline the principles of metallurgy: concentration, extraction, and purification of ores</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="periodic">1. Periodic Classification and Properties</h2>

<h3>Modern Periodic Table</h3>

<h4>Blocks, Groups, Periods</h4>
<p>The modern periodic table arranges elements in order of increasing atomic number. Elements are classified into four blocks s, p, d, f based on the subshell that receives the last electron. There are 18 groups and 7 periods. Group 1 to 2 constitute the s-block, Groups 13 to 18 the p-block, Groups 3 to 12 the d-block, and the two rows at the bottom are the f-block (lanthanoids and actinoids).</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Block</th><th>Groups</th><th>Subshell filling</th><th>Number of elements</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>s-block</td><td>1 to 2</td><td>ns(1 to 2)</td><td>14</td></tr>
<tr><td>p-block</td><td>13 to 18</td><td>np(1 to 6)</td><td>36</td></tr>
<tr><td>d-block</td><td>3 to 12</td><td>(n-1)d(1 to 10) ns(1 to 2)</td><td>40</td></tr>
<tr><td>f-block</td><td>---</td><td>(n-2)f(1 to 14)</td><td>28 (14+14)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="tip-box">
<div class="tip-title">Mnemonic: s-block elements</div>
<div class="tip-text">Group 1 (Alkali metals): <strong>Hi LiNa K Rb Cs Fr</strong> (H, Li, Na, K, Rb, Cs, Fr). Group 2 (Alkaline earth metals): <strong>Be Mg Ca Sr Ba Ra</strong> — Bana Manga Gaya Sri Lanka Baap Re.</div>
</div>

<h4>Periodic Law and Electronic Configurations</h4>
<p>Mendeleev periodic law stated that properties are a periodic function of atomic mass. The modern law states they are a periodic function of atomic number. The electronic configuration of an element determines its chemical properties. For example, all noble gases have a filled ns2 np6 configuration (except He: 1s2), making them chemically inert.</p>

<h3>Periodic Trends</h3>

<h4>Atomic Radius, Ionization Energy, Electronegativity</h4>
<p><strong>Atomic radius</strong> decreases across a period due to increased effective nuclear charge (Zeff) and increases down a group due to addition of new shells. <strong>Ionization energy (IE)</strong> is the energy required to remove the most loosely bound electron from an isolated gaseous atom. IE increases across a period and decreases down a group. Exceptions: Group 2 elements have higher IE than Group 13 due to stable s2 configuration; Group 15 has higher IE than Group 16 due to half-filled p3 stability.</p>
<p><strong>Electronegativity</strong> is the tendency of an atom to attract shared electrons. It increases across a period and decreases down a group. Fluorine is the most electronegative element (4.0 on Pauling scale).</p>

<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Across a Period (to the right)</th><th>Down a Group (downward)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Atomic radius</td><td>Decreases</td><td>Increases</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ionization energy</td><td>Increases</td><td>Decreases</td></tr>
<tr><td>Electronegativity</td><td>Increases</td><td>Decreases</td></tr>
<tr><td>Electron affinity</td><td>Increases (generally)</td><td>Decreases</td></tr>
<tr><td>Metallic character</td><td>Decreases</td><td>Increases</td></tr>
<tr><td>Non-metallic character</td><td>Increases</td><td>Decreases</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h4>Electron Affinity</h4>
<p>Electron affinity (EA) is the energy released when an electron is added to a neutral gaseous atom. Halogens have the highest electron affinity (Cl greater than F greater than Br greater than I). Chlorine has a higher EA than fluorine because fluorine small size causes electron-electron repulsion in its 2p subshell. For NEET, remember: EA of O less than S, and EA of N about zero (stable half-filled).</p>

<div class="tip-box">
<div class="tip-title">Trick: IE exceptions</div>
<div class="tip-text">Group 2 greater than Group 13 and Group 15 greater than Group 16 due to extra stability of half-filled or full-filled subshells. Example: Mg (738) greater than Al (578); P (1012) greater than S (1000).</div>
</div>

<h3>Anomalous Properties</h3>
<p>The first element of each group (Li, Be, B, C, N, O, F) shows anomalous behaviour due to small size, high electronegativity, high ionization energy, and absence of d-orbitals. For example, Li is harder than other alkali metals, BeO is amphoteric while MgO is basic, and boron is electron-deficient (forms covalent compounds unlike other group 13 elements which are metallic).</p>

<div class="tip-box">
<div class="tip-title">Key Mnemonic: Diagonal Relationship</div>
<div class="tip-text">Li similar to Mg, Be similar to Al, B similar to Si. This occurs because the charge or size ratio of the second-period element resembles the third-period element diagonally below it. Example: LiOH and Mg(OH)2 are both weak bases; BeO and Al2O3 are both amphoteric.</div>
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<div class="ex-title">Example 1</div>
<div class="ex-text">Arrange the following in order of increasing ionization energy: Na, Mg, Al, Si, P, S, Cl, Ar.</div>
<div class="ex-soln"><strong>Solution:</strong> Across period 3, IE increases generally: Na less than Al less than Mg less than Si less than S less than P less than Cl less than Ar. Exceptions: Mg (3s2) greater than Al (3s2 3p1) and P (3p3) greater than S (3p4).</div>
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<div class="ex-title">Example 2</div>
<div class="ex-text">Which element has the highest electron affinity in the periodic table? a) F b) Cl c) Br d) I</div>
<div class="ex-soln"><strong>Solution:</strong> Chlorine (b). Although fluorine is the most electronegative, its small size causes inter-electronic repulsion, reducing its electron affinity. Chlorine has the highest EA (-349 kJ per mol).</div>
</div>

<h2 id="hydrogen">2. Hydrogen</h2>

<h3>Position in Periodic Table</h3>
<p>Hydrogen resembles both alkali metals (Group 1) and halogens (Group 17). It has one electron like alkali metals (forms H+) but also gains an electron to form H- like halogens. Its unique behaviour arises from its very small size and high ionization energy (1312 kJ per mol). Hydrogen is placed separately in the periodic table because it does not fit perfectly into any group.</p>

<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Resemblance with alkali metals</th><th>Resemblance with halogens</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Electronic config</td><td>1s1 (ns1)</td><td>1s1 (needs one e-)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ion formation</td><td>H goes to H+ + e-</td><td>H + e- goes to H-</td></tr>
<tr><td>Oxidation state</td><td>+1</td><td>-1 (in hydrides)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Combination</td><td>Forms oxides, halides</td><td>Diatomic molecule (H2)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h3>Water and Hydrides</h3>
<p><strong>Hydrides</strong> are binary compounds of hydrogen. They are classified as ionic (s-block), covalent (p-block), and metallic (d- and f-block). Ionic hydrides (NaH, CaH2) react with water to produce H2 gas. Covalent hydrides (CH4, NH3, H2O, HF) show trends in boiling points: H2O, NH3, HF have anomalously high boiling points due to hydrogen bonding.</p>

<p><strong>Water:</strong> A universal solvent with high specific heat capacity (4.18 J per g per deg C). Its boiling point (100 deg C) is much higher than expected due to extensive hydrogen bonding. Hard water contains Ca2+ and Mg2+ ions; temporary hardness is removed by boiling, permanent hardness by washing soda (Na2CO3) or ion exchange.</p>

<h3>Hydrogen Peroxide (H2O2)</h3>
<p>Hydrogen peroxide has a non-planar, open-book structure with a dihedral angle of 111.5 degrees (gas phase). It acts as both an oxidising and reducing agent. Its important reactions: 2 H2O2 goes to 2 H2O + O2 (decomposition); H2O2 + 2 I- + 2 H+ goes to I2 + 2 H2O (oxidation); 2 MnO4- + 5 H2O2 + 6 H+ goes to 2 Mn2+ + 8 H2O + 5 O2 (reduction in acidic medium).</p>

<h3>Heavy Water (D2O)</h3>
<p>Heavy water is deuterium oxide. It is used as a moderator in nuclear reactors. Its physical constants differ from ordinary water: density (1.105 g per mL), melting point (3.82 deg C), boiling point (101.42 deg C).</p>

<div class="tip-box">
<div class="tip-title">Mnemonic: Hardness removal</div>
<div class="tip-text">Temporary hardness to boiling (Ca(HCO3)2 goes to CaCO3 + H2O + CO2). Permanent to washing soda (Na2CO3) or ion exchange resin.</div>
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<div class="ex-title">Example 3</div>
<div class="ex-text">Which of the following hydrides is basic? a) NH3 b) CH4 c) H2O d) HF</div>
<div class="ex-soln"><strong>Solution:</strong> NH3 (a) accepts a proton to form NH4+, making it a Bronsted base. CH4 is neutral, H2O is amphoteric, HF is acidic.</div>
</div>

<h2 id="s-block">3. s-Block Elements (Alkali and Alkaline Earth Metals)</h2>

<h3>General Characteristics</h3>
<p><strong>Alkali metals (Group 1):</strong> Li, Na, K, Rb, Cs, Fr. They are soft, highly reactive metals with low ionization energy, low melting points, and strong reducing power. Reactivity increases down the group. They form ionic hydrides (M+ H-), oxides (M2O, M2O2, MO2 depending on metal), and hydroxides (MOH as strong bases).</p>
<p><strong>Alkaline earth metals (Group 2):</strong> Be, Mg, Ca, Sr, Ba, Ra. They are harder, have higher melting points, and are less reactive than alkali metals. Be and Mg form covalent compounds due to high polarising power. Ca, Sr, Ba form ionic compounds.</p>

<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Alkali metals (Group 1)</th><th>Alkaline earth metals (Group 2)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Electronic config</td><td>ns1</td><td>ns2</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ionization energy</td><td>Low (decreases downward)</td><td>Higher than Group 1</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nature of oxides</td><td>Strongly basic</td><td>Basic (BeO amphoteric)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flame colour</td><td>Li (crimson), Na (yellow), K (violet), Rb (red-violet)</td><td>Ca (brick red), Sr (crimson), Ba (apple green)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reaction with water</td><td>Vigorous (K, Rb, Cs explode)</td><td>Slow (Be, Mg); vigorous (Ca, Sr, Ba)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hydration enthalpy</td><td>Li+ greater than Na+ greater than K+ greater than Rb+ greater than Cs+</td><td>Be2+ greater than Mg2+ greater than Ca2+ greater than Sr2+ greater than Ba2+</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h3>Anomalous Behaviour</h3>
<p><strong>Lithium</strong> shows anomalous behaviour: it is harder, has higher melting point, forms Li2O (not Li2O2), and LiOH is a weak base. Lithium alone forms Li3N (with N2) while other alkali metals do not. <strong>Beryllium</strong> is also anomalous: it forms covalent compounds, its oxide and hydroxide are amphoteric, and it does not liberate H2 from acids readily.</p>

<h3>Important Compounds</h3>
<p><strong>Sodium carbonate (Na2CO3):</strong> Manufactured by Solvay process. Used in glass, soap, and paper industries. <strong>Sodium hydroxide (NaOH):</strong> Manufactured by electrolysis of brine (chlor-alkali process). <strong>Sodium bicarbonate (NaHCO3):</strong> Used in baking soda, antacids. <strong>Calcium oxide (CaO):</strong> Quicklime, used in cement. <strong>Calcium hydroxide (Ca(OH)2):</strong> Slaked lime, used for whitewashing. <strong>Plaster of Paris (CaSO4 half H2O):</strong> Used in orthopaedics and construction.</p>

<div class="tip-box">
<div class="tip-title">Mnemonic: Flame colours</div>
<div class="tip-text">Li (crimson Li), Na (yellow Na), K (violet Potassium), Rb (red-Rb), Cs (blue-Cs). For Group 2: Ca (brick red Ca), Sr (crimson Sr), Ba (apple green Ba).</div>
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<div class="ex-title">Example 4</div>
<div class="ex-text">Which of the following alkali metals forms a superoxide? a) Li b) Na c) K d) All of these</div>
<div class="ex-soln"><strong>Solution:</strong> K, Rb, Cs form superoxides (MO2). Li forms Li2O, Na forms Na2O2. The stability of superoxides increases down the group due to the larger size of the cation stabilizing the large O2- anion. Hence option (c).</div>
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<div class="ex-title">Example 5</div>
<div class="ex-text">Be shows diagonal relationship with which element? a) Al b) Mg c) Li d) B</div>
<div class="ex-soln"><strong>Solution:</strong> Be shows diagonal relationship with Al (a). Both form amphoteric oxides and hydroxides, form covalent chlorides (BeCl2, AlCl3), and their carbides give methane on hydrolysis.</div>
</div>

<h2 id="p-block">4. p-Block Elements</h2>

<h3>Group 13 (Boron Family)</h3>
<p>B, Al, Ga, In, Tl. Boron is a metalloid; others are metals. The inert-pair effect (reluctance of s-electrons to participate in bonding) becomes prominent down the group: Tl shows +1 oxidation state more stable than +3. Boron forms electron-deficient compounds like BF3 (trigonal planar) and BCl3. Borax (Na2B4O7 10 H2O) is an important boron compound. Diborane (B2H6) has hydrogen-bridge bonding (3-centre-2-electron bonds).</p>

<h3>Group 14 (Carbon Family)</h3>
<p>C, Si, Ge, Sn, Pb. Carbon exhibits catenation (self-linking) due to strong C to C bonds. Silicon and germanium are semiconductors. Allotropes of carbon: diamond (sp3, hardest natural substance), graphite (sp2, conductor, lubricant), fullerenes (C60, soccer-ball structure). CO2 is linear; SiO2 is a 3D network solid. Inert-pair effect: Pb(II) is stable, Pb(IV) is strongly oxidising.</p>

<h3>Group 15 (Nitrogen Family)</h3>
<p>N, P, As, Sb, Bi. Nitrogen is a diatomic gas (N triple bond N, very stable). Phosphorus exists as P4 (white), red phosphorus, and black phosphorus. Ammonia (NH3) is a Lewis base with a trigonal pyramidal shape. HNO3 (nitric acid) is a strong oxidising agent. Phosphine (PH3) is less basic than NH3. Compounds: PCl5 (trigonal bipyramidal), PCl3 (pyramidal).</p>

<h3>Group 16 (Oxygen Family)</h3>
<p>O, S, Se, Te, Po. Ozone (O3) is an allotrope of oxygen, bent shape, acts as a bleaching agent. Sulphur exists as S8 (crown structure). Allotropes: rhombic (alpha), monoclinic (beta). H2SO4 (sulphuric acid, Oil of Vitriol) is manufactured by Contact process. It is a strong dehydrating and oxidising agent. SO2 is a reducing agent (decolourises KMnO4).</p>

<h3>Group 17 (Halogens)</h3>
<p>F, Cl, Br, I, At. Halogens are diatomic molecules with increasing bond dissociation energy: Cl2 greater than Br2 greater than F2 greater than I2 (F to F bond is weak due to lone pair repulsion). Oxidising power: F2 greater than Cl2 greater than Br2 greater than I2. Disproportionation reactions: Cl2 + 2 NaOH goes to NaCl + NaOCl + H2O (cold); 3 Cl2 + 6 NaOH goes to 5 NaCl + NaClO3 + 3 H2O (hot). Interhalogen compounds (ClF, BrF5, IF7) are formed by direct combination.</p>

<h3>Group 18 (Noble Gases)</h3>
<p>He, Ne, Ar, Kr, Xe, Rn. Monatomic gases with complete octet (except He: 2). Their inertness decreases down the group. Xenon forms compounds with F and O: XeF2 (linear), XeF4 (square planar), XeF6 (distorted octahedral), XeO3 (trigonal pyramidal). Uses: He in balloons, Ne in discharge tubes, Ar as inert atmosphere, Xe in flash lamps.</p>

<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>General electronic config</th><th>First element</th><th>Key trend</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>13 (Boron family)</td><td>ns2 np1</td><td>B (metalloid)</td><td>Inert-pair effect: +1 stable for Tl</td></tr>
<tr><td>14 (Carbon family)</td><td>ns2 np2</td><td>C (non-metal)</td><td>Catenation decreases downward; Pb(IV) oxidising</td></tr>
<tr><td>15 (Nitrogen family)</td><td>ns2 np3</td><td>N (non-metal)</td><td>Basicity of hydrides decreases downward</td></tr>
<tr><td>16 (Oxygen family)</td><td>ns2 np4</td><td>O (non-metal)</td><td>Oxidising power decreases downward</td></tr>
<tr><td>17 (Halogens)</td><td>ns2 np5</td><td>F (most reactive)</td><td>Electron affinity: Cl greater than F</td></tr>
<tr><td>18 (Noble gases)</td><td>ns2 np6</td><td>He (inert)</td><td>Xe forms most compounds</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="tip-box">
<div class="tip-title">Mnemonic: p-block periodic table groups</div>
<div class="tip-text">Group 13: B Al Ga In Tl as <strong>Baba Aloo Gajar In Tel</strong><br>Group 14: C Si Ge Sn Pb as <strong>Chemistry Sir Gives Some Problems</strong><br>Group 15: N P As Sb Bi as <strong>New Policies As Soon Bhi</strong><br>Group 16: O S Se Te Po as <strong>Old Sir Sell Telescope Poorly</strong><br>Group 17: F Cl Br I At as <strong>Fully Clear Bromine In Atom</strong></div>
</div>

<div class="tip-box">
<div class="tip-title">Trick: Inert-pair effect</div>
<div class="tip-text">Down a p-block group, the +2 (for group 14) or +1 (for group 13) oxidation state becomes more stable than the group oxidation state. Example: PbO2 is a strong oxidising agent (tends to reduce to Pb2+). Tl+ is more stable than Tl3+.</div>
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<div class="ex-title">Example 6</div>
<div class="ex-text">Which of the following has a linear structure? a) XeF2 b) XeF4 c) XeF6 d) XeO3</div>
<div class="ex-soln"><strong>Solution:</strong> XeF2 (a) has sp3d hybridization with 3 lone pairs, giving a linear shape. XeF4 is square planar, XeF6 is distorted octahedral, XeO3 is trigonal pyramidal.</div>
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<div class="ex-title">Example 7</div>
<div class="ex-text">Which halogen does NOT show positive oxidation state? a) F b) Cl c) Br d) I</div>
<div class="ex-soln"><strong>Solution:</strong> Fluorine (a) is the most electronegative element and always shows -1 oxidation state. It does not have d-orbitals, so it cannot expand its octet. Cl, Br, I show +1, +3, +5, +7 oxidation states.</div>
</div>

<h2 id="d-block">5. d- and f-Block Elements</h2>

<h3>Transition Elements (d-block)</h3>

<h4>Electronic Configurations, Oxidation States</h4>
<p>Transition elements are defined as elements that have partially filled d-orbitals in their ground or common oxidation states. General electronic configuration: (n-1)d(1 to 10) ns(1 to 2). The three transition series correspond to the filling of 3d, 4d, and 5d orbitals. Zn, Cd, Hg are not transition metals as they have fully filled d10 configurations in both ground and common oxidation states.</p>

<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Atomic No.</th><th>Electronic configuration</th><th>Common oxidation states</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Sc</td><td>21</td><td>(Ar) 3d1 4s2</td><td>+3</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ti</td><td>22</td><td>(Ar) 3d2 4s2</td><td>+3, +4</td></tr>
<tr><td>V</td><td>23</td><td>(Ar) 3d3 4s2</td><td>+2, +3, +4, +5</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cr</td><td>24</td><td>(Ar) 3d5 4s1</td><td>+2, +3, +6</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mn</td><td>25</td><td>(Ar) 3d5 4s2</td><td>+2, +4, +6, +7</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fe</td><td>26</td><td>(Ar) 3d6 4s2</td><td>+2, +3</td></tr>
<tr><td>Co</td><td>27</td><td>(Ar) 3d7 4s2</td><td>+2, +3</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ni</td><td>28</td><td>(Ar) 3d8 4s2</td><td>+2</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cu</td><td>29</td><td>(Ar) 3d10 4s1</td><td>+1, +2</td></tr>
<tr><td>Zn</td><td>30</td><td>(Ar) 3d10 4s2</td><td>+2</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="tip-box">
<div class="tip-title">Mnemonic: First transition series</div>
<div class="tip-text"><strong>Sc Ti V Cr Mn Fe Co Ni Cu Zn</strong> as <em>Science Teacher Vinay Creates Many Fun Chemistry Notes for Class Using Zinc</em>. Remember exceptions: Cr (3d5 4s1) and Cu (3d10 4s1) due to half-filled and fully-filled d-subshell stability.</div>
</div>

<h4>Colour, Magnetic and Catalytic Properties</h4>
<p><strong>Colour:</strong> Transition metal ions are coloured due to d to d transitions of electrons. In the presence of ligands, d-orbitals split into two sets (t2g and eg). The energy difference (delta) corresponds to the wavelength of visible light absorbed. Example: Ti3+ (purple), Cr3+ (green), Mn2+ (pale pink), Fe3+ (yellow), Co2+ (pink), Ni2+ (green), Cu2+ (blue).</p>
<p><strong>Magnetic properties:</strong> Paramagnetism arises from unpaired electrons. The magnetic moment mu = sqrt(n(n+2)) BM (where n = number of unpaired electrons). Diamagnetic species have all electrons paired. <strong>Catalytic properties:</strong> Transition metals (Fe, Ni, Pt, Pd) and their compounds are excellent catalysts due to variable oxidation states and ability to form intermediate complexes. Examples: Fe in Haber process (NH3), V2O5 in Contact process (H2SO4), Ni in hydrogenation.</p>

<div class="tip-box">
<div class="tip-title">Trick: Magnetic moment calculation</div>
<div class="tip-text">For NEET, mu = sqrt(n(n+2)) BM. n = number of unpaired electrons. For Mn2+ (d5), n=5, mu = sqrt(35) = 5.92 BM. For Fe3+ (d5), same value. For Cu2+ (d9), n=1, mu = sqrt(3) = 1.73 BM.</div>
</div>

<h3>Lanthanoids and Actinoids</h3>
<p><strong>Lanthanoids (4f series):</strong> Ce (58) to Lu (71). They exhibit lanthanoid contraction which is a steady decrease in ionic radius with increasing atomic number due to poor shielding of 4f electrons. This leads to similar chemical properties and difficulty in separation. Common oxidation state: +3 (Ce also shows +4, Eu shows +2).</p>
<p><strong>Actinoids (5f series):</strong> Th (90) to Lr (103). They are radioactive. Uranium (U), Plutonium (Pu) are used in nuclear reactors. Actinoids show greater range of oxidation states than lanthanoids (up to +7). The 5f orbitals are more diffuse and participate in bonding.</p>

<div class="example-box">
<div class="ex-title">Example 8</div>
<div class="ex-text">The spin-only magnetic moment of Fe3+ in BM is: a) 4.90 b) 5.92 c) 3.87 d) 2.84</div>
<div class="ex-soln"><strong>Solution:</strong> Fe3+ has electronic configuration (Ar) 3d5. Number of unpaired electrons n = 5. mu = sqrt(5(5+2)) = sqrt(35) = 5.92 BM. Hence option (b) is correct.</div>
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<div class="ex-title">Example 9</div>
<div class="ex-text">Which of the following is not a transition element? a) Mn b) Cu c) Zn d) Fe</div>
<div class="ex-soln"><strong>Solution:</strong> Zn (c) has d10 configuration in both ground state (3d10 4s2) and common +2 oxidation state (3d10). It does not have partially filled d-orbitals, so it is not a transition element. Cu (d10 in +1 but d9 in +2) is a transition element.</div>
</div>

<h2 id="coordination">6. Coordination Compounds</h2>

<h3>Werner Theory</h3>
<p>Alfred Werner proposed that metal ions have two types of valencies: primary (ionizable) and secondary (non-ionizable). Primary valency corresponds to oxidation state; secondary valency corresponds to coordination number. For CoCl3.6NH3 (Co(NH3)6 Cl3), Co has primary valency 3 and secondary valency 6. Modern theory uses the concept of coordinate covalent bonds between the metal (Lewis acid) and ligands (Lewis bases).</p>

<h3>IUPAC Nomenclature</h3>
<p>Rules: 1) Cation named before anion. 2) Ligands named in alphabetical order before metal. 3) Anionic ligands end in -o (Cl- goes to chloro, CN- goes to cyano). 4) Neutral ligands retain name (NH3 goes to ammine, H2O goes to aqua). 5) Oxidation state of metal shown in Roman numerals in parentheses. 6) For anionic complex, metal name ends in -ate.</p>

<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Formula</th><th>IUPAC Name</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>(Co(NH3)6)Cl3</td><td>Hexaamminecobalt(III) chloride</td></tr>
<tr><td>K4(Fe(CN)6)</td><td>Potassium hexacyanoferrate(II)</td></tr>
<tr><td>(Cr(H2O)6)Cl3</td><td>Hexaaquachromium(III) chloride</td></tr>
<tr><td>(Ni(CO)4)</td><td>Tetracarbonylnickel(0)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Na(Al(OH)4)</td><td>Sodium tetrahydroxoaluminate(III)</td></tr>
<tr><td>(Fe(C2O4)3)3-</td><td>Trioxalatoferrate(III) ion</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h3>Isomerism</h3>
<p><strong>Structural isomerism:</strong> Ionization (e.g., (Co(NH3)5Br)SO4 vs (Co(NH3)5SO4)Br), linkage (ambidentate ligands: NO2- as nitro vs nitrito), coordination, and hydrate isomerism.</p>
<p><strong>Stereoisomerism:</strong> Geometrical (cis-trans in square planar (Pt(NH3)2Cl2); cis-trans and fac-mer in octahedral) and optical isomerism (non-superimposable mirror images, e.g., (Co(en)3)3+).</p>

<h3>Valence Bond Theory (VBT) and Crystal Field Theory (CFT)</h3>
<p><strong>VBT:</strong> Metal ion uses hybrid orbitals to accept lone pairs from ligands. Inner orbital (low spin) complexes use (n-1)d orbitals for hybridization (e.g., d2sp3). Outer orbital (high spin) complexes use nd orbitals (e.g., sp3d2). Example: (CoF6)3- (high spin, paramagnetic, sp3d2) vs (Co(NH3)6)3+ (low spin, diamagnetic, d2sp3).</p>
<p><strong>CFT:</strong> In octahedral field, d-orbitals split into t2g (dxy, dyz, dzx) which is lower energy, and eg (dx2-y2, dz2) which is higher energy. The splitting parameter (delta o) depends on ligand field strength: weak field ligands (I-, Br-, Cl-, F-) go to small delta to high spin; strong field ligands (CO, CN-, NH3, en) go to large delta to low spin. Spectrochemical series: I- less than Br- less than Cl- less than F- less than OH- less than H2O less than NH3 less than en less than NO2- less than CN- less than CO.</p>

<div class="tip-box">
<div class="tip-title">Mnemonic: Spectrochemical series</div>
<div class="tip-text"><strong>I Br Cl F O H H N C</strong> as <em>Iodine Brought Chlorine Fluorine Over Here, Having Nice Coffee</em>. I- less than Br- less than Cl- less than F- less than OH- less than H2O less than NH3 less than en less than NO2- less than CN- less than CO. Remember: CO is the strongest field ligand.</div>
</div>

<h3>Stability and Applications</h3>
<p>Stability of coordination complexes depends on: 1) Charge on metal ion (higher charge gives more stable), 2) Nature of ligand (chelating ligands form more stable complexes due to chelate effect), 3) Size of the metal ion. Applications: EDTA is used in treating heavy metal poisoning; (Pt(NH3)2Cl2) (cis-platin) is an anticancer drug; coordination complexes are used in electroplating, water softening, and as catalysts.</p>

<div class="example-box">
<div class="ex-title">Example 10</div>
<div class="ex-text">The coordination number and oxidation state of Co in (Co(NH3)4Cl2)Cl are: a) 6, +3 b) 4, +2 c) 6, +2 d) 4, +3</div>
<div class="ex-soln"><strong>Solution:</strong> The complex is (Co(NH3)4Cl2)Cl. One Cl is outside coordination sphere. Inside, 4 NH3 (neutral) + 2 Cl-. CN = 4 + 2 = 6. Let x = oxidation state of Co. x + 0(4) + (-1)(2) = +1 (overall charge). x - 2 = +1, x = +3. Hence option (a).</div>
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<div class="ex-title">Example 11</div>
<div class="ex-text">Which of the following is a low-spin complex? a) (CoF6)3- b) (Co(NH3)6)3+ c) (FeF6)3- d) (Fe(H2O)6)2+</div>
<div class="ex-soln"><strong>Solution:</strong> (Co(NH3)6)3+ (b). NH3 is a strong field ligand, causing large delta o. Co3+ (d6) in strong field pairs electrons, giving t2g6 eg0 (low spin, diamagnetic). (CoF6)3- and (FeF6)3- are high spin (F- is weak field).</div>
</div>

<h2 id="metallurgy">7. Metallurgy</h2>

<h3>Principles of Metallurgy</h3>
<p>Metallurgy is the process of extracting metals from their ores. It involves three main stages:</p>
<p><strong>1. Concentration of ore:</strong> Removal of gangue from ore. Methods include hydraulic washing (gravity separation), magnetic separation, froth flotation (for sulphide ores), and leaching (chemical method, e.g., cyanide process for Au or Ag, Bayer process for Al).</p>
<p><strong>2. Extraction of metal:</strong> Reduction of the concentrated ore to the free metal. Methods: Calcination (heating in limited air, e.g., ZnCO3 goes to ZnO + CO2), Roasting (heating in excess air, e.g., 2 ZnS + 3 O2 goes to 2 ZnO + 2 SO2), Smelting (reduction using carbon, e.g., Fe2O3 + 3 C goes to 2 Fe + 3 CO), and electrolytic reduction (e.g., Hall-Heroult process for Al, Down process for Na).</p>
<p><strong>3. Purification:</strong> Distillation (for Zn, Hg), liquation (for Sn, Pb), electrolytic refining (for Cu, Ag, Au), zone refining (for Si, Ge as semiconductors), vapour phase refining (Mond process for Ni, Van Arkel process for Ti).</p>

<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Metal</th><th>Ore</th><th>Concentration method</th><th>Extraction method</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Iron</td><td>Haematite (Fe2O3)</td><td>Magnetic separation</td><td>Smelting in blast furnace</td></tr>
<tr><td>Aluminium</td><td>Bauxite (Al2O3.2H2O)</td><td>Bayer process (leaching)</td><td>Hall-Heroult (electrolysis)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Copper</td><td>Copper pyrite (CuFeS2)</td><td>Froth flotation</td><td>Roasting + smelting + Bessemer</td></tr>
<tr><td>Zinc</td><td>Zinc blende (ZnS)</td><td>Froth flotation</td><td>Roasting + reduction + distillation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Silver</td><td>Argentite (Ag2S)</td><td>Cyanide leaching</td><td>Reduction with Zn</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gold</td><td>Native or Quartz</td><td>Cyanide leaching</td><td>Reduction with Zn</td></tr>
<tr><td>Titanium</td><td>Rutile (TiO2)</td><td>---</td><td>Van Arkel (vapour phase)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nickel</td><td>Nickel oxide</td><td>---</td><td>Mond process (vapour phase)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="tip-box">
<div class="tip-title">Mnemonic: Metallurgy order</div>
<div class="tip-text"><strong>Concentrate, Extract, Purify</strong> as <em>Come Eat Pasta</em>. For specific processes: Froth flotation for sulphide ores; Leaching for Au, Ag, Al; Electrolytic refining for Cu, Ag, Au.</div>
</div>

<div class="tip-box">
<div class="tip-title">Trick: Reducing agents in metallurgy</div>
<div class="tip-text">Carbon is used for moderately reactive metals (Fe, Zn, Cu). Electrolysis is used for highly reactive metals (Na, K, Mg, Al, Ca). Thermite reduction (Al as reducing agent) is used for Cr, Mn. Self-reduction (auto-reduction) occurs for Cu2S + O2 goes to 2 Cu + SO2.</div>
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<div class="ex-title">Example 12</div>
<div class="ex-text">In the extraction of copper from copper pyrite, the Bessemer converter is used for: a) Roasting b) Smelting c) Reduction d) Self-reduction</div>
<div class="ex-soln"><strong>Solution:</strong> The Bessemer converter is used for self-reduction (d). In the converter, Cu2S reacts with O2 to form Cu2O and SO2. Cu2O then reacts with remaining Cu2S to give blister copper: 2 Cu2S + 3 O2 goes to 2 Cu2O + 2 SO2; 2 Cu2O + Cu2S goes to 6 Cu + SO2.</div>
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<div class="ex-title">Example 13</div>
<div class="ex-text">Which process is used for the purification of titanium? a) Mond process b) Van Arkel process c) Zone refining d) Cupellation</div>
<div class="ex-soln"><strong>Solution:</strong> Van Arkel process (b). Titanium is heated with iodine to form volatile TiI4, which decomposes on a hot tungsten filament to deposit pure Ti. Mond process is for Ni, zone refining for Si or Ge.</div>
</div>

                </div>
